    "https://umd.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UMES/job/University-of-Maryland-Eastern-Shore/Pharmacy-Practice-and-Administration-Lecturer--Part-time-_JR101992": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty: Pharmacy Practice and Administration",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\n\nJob Description Summary Organization's Summary Statement: The School of Pharmacy at the University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) invites applicants to apply for an Adjunct faculty position in the areas of Pharmacy Practice and Administration. UMES is located in rural Maryland and is one of America’s most beautiful campuses. The School of Pharmacy is dedicated to developing exemplary pharmacy professionals and scholars who are committed to patient-centered care, lifelong learning, discovery, and service for diverse communities of the Delmarva Peninsula, the State of Maryland, and around the world. Faculty are dedicated and promote a positive work environment. The UMES School of Pharmacy utilizes a three-year, year-round, modular curriculum. Courses in our curriculum are team-taught. Salary: $1,800 - $3,417.53 (Courses are team taught, so faculty will not be teaching the entire course on their own) Benefits: UMES offers exciting opportunities for professional growth. Generous benefits include medical, prescription, dental, and accidental death & dismemberment. The medical benefits are subject to subsidy restrictions based upon number of courses taught.  Voluntary participation in supplemental retirement accounts (SRAs) to include TIAA, Fidelity, or MSRP-Nationwide; participation in state sponsored MetLife life insurance and/or USM sponsored MetLife life insurance plan; long term disability insurance, and the employee assistance program. Responsibilities: •Teach and develop courses in pharmacy practice. •Create assessment questions and other classroom activities. •Performs other related duties as assigned. Qualifications: •Doctor of Pharmacy •Post-graduate residency/fellowship training or equivalent experience •Experience in Academia Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: •A commitment to teaching a diverse population of graduate students at the university level. •Ability to work effectively and collegially with faculty and staff in and of the School of Pharmacy Physical Demands: •May require extended periods of standing, bending, sitting at desk. •May require lifting up to 25 lbs. •Requires communication with a variety of constituents externally and internally. •Requires operation of a variety of office equipment. Preferences: • Expertise in pharmacy law, hospital pharmacy, or community pharmacy. Minimum Qualifications The title Lecturer will ordinarily be used to designate appointments of persons who are serving in a teaching capacity for a limited time or part-time. The normal requirement is a Master's degree in the field of instruction or a related field, or equivalent professional experience in the field of instruction. Appointments to this rank are typically one to three years and are renewable. Additional Job Details Required Application Materials: Qualified applicants should submit a cover letter, resume, and the names of three current professional references (current or past supervisor) including e-mail address, and telephone number (current supervisor will not be contacted without the candidate’s prior consent). All applicants must apply using the new online application system. Please visit https://umd.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UMES to apply. The successful candidate must be able to show acceptable documentation establishing the right to accept employment in the United States of America without employer sponsorship.   Best Consideration Date: N/A   Posting Close Date: N/A   Open Until Filled: Yes   Department UMES-HLPR-School of Health Professions Worker Sub-Type Faculty Non-Regular (Fixed Term) Salary Range $1,800 - $3,417.53 (Courses are team taught, so faculty will not be teaching the entire course on their own) EEO Statement UMES  is an EEO/AA employer and will not discriminate against any employee or applicant because of race, age, sex, color, sexual orientation, religion, national origin, marital status, genetic information, or political affiliation. Minorities, women, veterans, and persons with disabilities are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate must be able to show acceptable documentation establishing the right to accept employment in the United States of America without employer sponsorship. Diversity Statement The University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) is an equal access, equal opportunity institution fully committed to diversity in education and employment. All students, employees, and the campus community at UMES are valued, respected, and have the opportunity to receive an equitable experience in an inclusive, welcoming environment of openness and appreciation. Title IX Statement It is the policy of the University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) to comply with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, which prohibits discrimination (including sexual harassment and sexual violence) based on sex in the University's educational programs and activities. Title IX also prohibits retaliation for asserting or otherwise participating in claims of sex discriminations or harassment."
    },
    "https://umd.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UMES/job/University-of-Maryland-Eastern-Shore/Assistant-Associate-Professor--Animal-Health---Infectious-Diseases_JR104781": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor  –Animal Health & Infectious Diseases",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description Summary Organization's Summary Statement: The University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) School of Veterinary Medicine, in coordination with UMES Extension and the UMES Agricultural Experiment Station, seeks applications for a Veterinarian at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor. This position is a twelve-month, tenure-track appointment with an effort distribution of approximately 50% Research and 50% Extension/Service/ Teaching. The position will have multiple reporting responsibilities to the Dean SVM, Director UMES AES, and Administrator UMES Extension. This position emphasizes research and service in the veterinary and animal-health sciences, with a disciplinary focus on one or more of the following areas: infectious diseases, parasitology, virology, bacteriology, immunology, biosecurity, and epidemiology. The successful candidate will build an extramurally funded research program and deliver science-based Extension and diagnostic service that protects animal and public health across the Delmarva Peninsula, the state of Maryland, and beyond, consistent with the School’s One Health mission. The developing School of Veterinary Medicine — the first within Maryland — is committed to advancing the health of animals, humans, and the environment while supporting a thriving regional agricultural economy and expanding diversity in the veterinary profession. The successful candidate will help shape the School’s research and Extension enterprise and contribute to the education of veterinary and graduate students. University Information The University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) is the 1890 Land-Grant University in Maryland and a historically Black university, located in a rural setting on the Delmarva Peninsula between the Atlantic Ocean and the Chesapeake Bay. The major metropolitan areas of Washington, D.C., Baltimore, MD, and Philadelphia, PA, are within easy travel distance. The region is one of the nation’s most important animal-agriculture areas, anchored by a major commercial poultry industry and a diverse base of small ruminant, cattle, swine, and small-farm producers. UMES Extension and the university’s 365-acre Research, Extension and Teaching Farm provide an exceptional platform for applied, producer-engaged scholarship. Salary Range: $135,000 – $190,000 annually (commensurate with academic rank and experience). Benefits: The University of Maryland Eastern Shore, as a University System of Maryland institution and a Maryland State agency, provides a comprehensive benefits package to all regular faculty and staff. The Office of Employee Benefits provides consultative services and assistance with benefits planning, enrollment, and changes. Eligible employees may participate in a variety of benefit plans including: • Health insurance, including multiple medical plan options for employees and dependents offered through the State of Maryland Employee Benefits Division. • Prescription drug, dental, and flexible spending accounts, and vision coverage. • Retirement plans: mandatory enrollment in a state pension system or an Optional Retirement Plan with Fidelity or TIAA, Supplemental Retirement Accounts (TIAA, Fidelity, or MSRP-Nationwide) with $600 annual state match, Life Insurance (state sponsored MetLife, USM sponsored MetLife Plan, life insurance and long-term disability insurance). • Tuition remission benefits for eligible employees and, subject to USM policies, spouses and dependents at University System of Maryland institutions. • Exempt positions start at 22 days annual per year, sick leave 15 days per year, personal leave 3 days per year), and paid holidays. • Employee wellness and support resources and access to the State of Maryland wellness programs. The successful candidate will be primarily engaged in research (50%) and Extension/service/teaching appointment (50%). Research (approximately 50%): • Develop and sustain an independent and collaborative, extramurally funded research program in one or more focus disciplines — infectious diseases, parasitology, virology, bacteriology, immunology, biosecurity, and/or epidemiology — relevant to the animal industries and public health of the region. • Pursue intramural and extramural funding (e.g., USDA-NIFA, NIH, USDA- APHIS, industry, and foundation sources) and demonstrate strong grantsmanship. • Publish in peer-reviewed journals and disseminate findings at scientific and industry meetings; contribute to the School’s emerging research infrastructure, including diagnostic and laboratory capacity. • Mentor and train graduate and professional (DVM) students and research staff, and collaborate across disciplines with School of Veterinary Medicine, Department of Agriculture, Food and Resource Sciences, and Extension colleagues. Extension/Service/Teaching (approximately 50%): • Develop and deliver science-based Extension programs, diagnostic support, and outreach addressing infectious and zoonotic disease, parasitology, biosecurity, disease surveillance, and epidemiology for producers, veterinarians, agency partners, and communities. • Provide subject-matter expertise to state and federal animal-health agencies, producer organizations, and allied industry; contribute to disease surveillance, biosecurity planning, and emergency preparedness and response for animal-health events. • Develop high-quality educational resources (fact sheets, decision tools, online modules) and build collaborative relationships with UMES Extension, county educators, and regional partners. • Support the development and implementation of the new veterinary medical school program through didactic, laboratory, or clinical instruction as assigned. • Participate in student learning labs, pre-veterinary activities, advising, and mentoring; contribute to curriculum development and the School’s accreditation efforts. • Provide service to the School, University, profession, and community, and perform other related duties as assigned. Required Qualifications • Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) or equivalent professional degree from an accredited institution. • An earned or near-completion graduate degree (Ph.D. or M.S.) and/or advanced training in a relevant discipline (infectious diseases, parasitology, virology, bacteriology, immunology, biosecurity or epidemiology), OR equivalent research training and demonstrated scholarly productivity. • Strong evidence of scholarly activity and research productivity, and experience in grantsmanship. Physical Demands: • May require extended periods of standing, bending, stooping, and sitting at a desk. • May require lifting. • May involve work in laboratory, farm, and field settings, including contact with animals and biological samples. • Requires communication with a variety of constituents, externally and internally, and operation of a variety of office and laboratory equipment. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: • Strong organizational skills and the ability to work both collaboratively and independently. • Excellent written and oral communication skills and the ability to translate science for diverse producer, veterinary, agency, and community audiences. • A commitment to animal welfare, biosafety, and ethical research. • Ability to work flexible hours, including occasional weekends and holidays as needed for research, service, or emergency animal-health response. • Strong computer skills. Preferences: • Eligible for licensure to practice veterinary medicine in Maryland. • Board certification relevant to the position (e.g., ACVM, ACVPM, ACPV, or ACVP). • A record of extramural grant funding and peer-reviewed publication in one or more focus disciplines. • Experience in a veterinary diagnostic laboratory, disease-surveillance, or epidemiology setting. • Prior Extension, outreach, or adult-education experience across in-person, virtual, and written modalities. • Experience serving small, beginning, or historically underserved farmers and rural communities, and familiarity with the poultry and small-ruminant systems of the Delmarva Peninsula. Additional Job Details Required Application Materials: Review of applications will begin immediately. Qualified applicants should submit: Letter of interest outlining qualifications and leadership experience. Curriculum vitae. Contact information for three professional references. (current supervisor will not be contacted without the candidate's prior consent). All applicants must apply using the new online application system. Please visit   https://umd.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UMES   to apply. The successful candidate must be able to show acceptable documentation establishing the right to accept employment in the United States of America without employer sponsorship.   Best Consideration Date: N/A   Posting Close Date: N/A   Open Until Filled: Yes   Department UMES-SVM- School of Veterinary Medicine Worker Sub-Type Faculty Regular Salary Range $135,000 - $190,000 (commensurate with academic rank and experience) EEO Statement UMES  is an EEO/AA employer and will not discriminate against any employee or applicant because of race, age, sex, color, sexual orientation, religion, national origin, marital status, genetic information, or political affiliation. Minorities, women, veterans, and persons with disabilities are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate must be able to show acceptable documentation establishing the right to accept employment in the United States of America without employer sponsorship. Diversity Statement The University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) is an equal access, equal opportunity institution fully committed to diversity in education and employment. All students, employees, and the campus community at UMES are valued, respected, and have the opportunity to receive an equitable experience in an inclusive, welcoming environment of openness and appreciation. Title IX Statement It is the policy of the University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) to comply with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, which prohibits discrimination (including sexual harassment and sexual violence) based on sex in the University's educational programs and activities. Title IX also prohibits retaliation for asserting or otherwise participating in claims of sex discriminations or harassment."
    },
    "https://umd.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UMES/job/University-of-Maryland-Eastern-Shore/Assistant-Professor-in-Agricultural-Ecohydrology_JR105064": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Agricultural Ecohydrology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description Summary Organization's Summary Statement: Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Agricultural Ecohydrology Location: University of Maryland Eastern Shore, Princess Anne, MD Departments: Department of Natural Sciences, and Department of Agriculture, Food, and Resource Sciences, Simon A. Haley School of Agriculture and Natural Sciences Appointment Distribution: 50% Research, 35% Extension, 15% Teaching Start Date: Spring 2027 (flexible) The School of Agricultural and Natural Sciences (SANS) at the University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position in Agricultural Ecohydrology. We seek a creative and collaborative scientist working at the interface of water, soil, agriculture, nutrient management, biogeochemistry, and watershed processes, with particular interest in agricultural and coastal landscapes. This is a cross-departmental appointment, with the tenure home shared between the Department of Natural Sciences (60%, tenure home) and the Department of Agriculture, Food, and Resource Sciences (40%). Consistent with the position’s integrated research and Extension responsibilities, the successful candidate will also have reporting responsibilities to the UMES Agricultural Experiment Station and UMES Extension. The successful candidate will develop an applied research and Extension program addressing water-related challenges affecting agricultural production, natural resources, and water quality. The position is particularly well suited for a scientist interested in the Delmarva Peninsula and Chesapeake Bay watershed, where intensive agricultural production occurs within a low-relief coastal landscape characterized by extensive drainage networks, shallow groundwater, nutrient-sensitive waterways, wetlands, and increasing challenges associated with extreme precipitation, drought, sea-level rise, and salinity. This strategic hire will strengthen UMES capacity in agricultural water management, soil and water quality, climate-resilient agriculture, and watershed science in support of the University’s 1890 land-grant research, Extension, and teaching missions. The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate broadly across agricultural, environmental, engineering, and marine sciences and to translate fundamental understanding of hydrologic and biogeochemical processes into practical approaches for producers, land managers, agencies, and communities. Salary Range: $80,000 - $95,000 (Commensurate with qualifications and experience) Benefits: The University of Maryland Eastern Shore, as a University System of Maryland institution and a Maryland State agency, provides a comprehensive benefits package to all regular faculty and staff. The Office of Employee Benefits provides consultative services and assistance with benefits planning, enrollment, and changes. Eligible employees may participate in a variety of benefit plans including: • Health insurance, including multiple medical plan options for employees and dependents offered through the State of Maryland Employee Benefits Division. • Prescription drug, dental, and flexible spending accounts, and vision coverage. • Retirement plans: mandatory enrollment in a state pension system or an Optional Retirement Plan with Fidelity or TIAA, Supplemental Retirement Accounts (TIAA, Fidelity, or MSRP-Nationwide) with $600 annual state match, Life Insurance (state sponsored MetLife, USM sponsored MetLife Plan, life insurance and long-term disability insurance). • Tuition remission benefits for eligible employees and, subject to USM policies, spouses and dependents at University System of Maryland institutions. • Leave benefits, including paid annual (22 days per year) and sick leave (15 days), personal leave (3 days per year) and paid holidays. • Employee wellness and support resources and access to the State of Maryland wellness programs. Position Details: The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate Environmental Science education and to graduate programs in Marine, Estuarine, and Environmental Sciences (MEES), Toxicology, and Food and Agricultural Sciences (FASC) at UMES. Areas of Interest: We are particularly interested in candidates whose research or technical experience aligns with, but is not limited to, one or more of the following areas: • Agricultural hydrology and water management, including soil-water dynamics, crop water availability, irrigation, drainage, water-use efficiency, and interactions between agricultural management and hydrologic processes; • Agricultural nutrient transport and water quality, including movement and transformation of nitrogen, phosphorus, sediment, and other constituents through soils, groundwater, drainage systems, wetlands, streams, and receiving waters; • Soil health, climate resilience, and salinity in agricultural landscapes, including effects of management practices on infiltration, runoff, water storage, nutrient cycling, drought and flooding resilience, sea-level rise, and saltwater intrusion; • Watershed and landscape-scale agricultural hydrology, including surface water-groundwater interactions and linkages among farms, drainage networks, wetlands, streams, estuaries, and downstream water quality, particularly within the Delmarva Peninsula and Chesapeake Bay watershed; • Quantitative and predictive modeling of agricultural and ecohydrologic systems, including statistical, process-based, spatial, machine learning, and deep learning approaches to predict soil moisture, water availability, nutrient losses, water quality, crop stress, flooding, drought, salinity, and related responses; • Integration and analysis of field measurements, sensor networks, remote sensing, unmanned aerial systems, GIS, and complex spatial and temporal datasets using modern statistical, computational, and data-driven approaches; • Development of predictive and decision-support tools that translate agricultural, hydrologic, and environmental data into practical information for producers, Extension professionals, conservation agencies, and other stakeholders. Candidates are not expected to work across all of these areas. We are particularly interested in applicants who combine strong understanding of agricultural and hydrologic processes with rigorous quantitative analysis and modern modeling or data-driven approaches to address challenges relevant to the Delmarva Peninsula, Chesapeake Bay watershed, and agricultural systems more broadly. Responsibilities: The successful candidate will be expected to: • Develop nationally recognized, integrated research and Extension programs in agricultural ecohydrology addressing important agricultural, water- resource, and environmental challenges, including a strong externally funded research portfolio; • Conduct applied and translational research that advances agricultural productivity, water and nutrient-use efficiency, soil and water conservation, environmental stewardship, and resilience of agricultural systems; • Establish field-based research relevant to agricultural landscapes of Maryland, the Delmarva Peninsula, and the Chesapeake Bay watershed, while developing a program with regional and national significance; • Develop and deliver an Extension program in agricultural ecohydrology and collaborate with UMES Extension professionals to translate research findings into practical information, demonstrations, decision-support resources, and educational programs for farmers, agricultural professionals, conservation practitioners, agencies, and other stakeholders; • Engage in interdisciplinary teams across SANS and beyond, integrating hydrology with soil science, agronomy, crop science, environmental science, engineering, geospatial science, climate science, and related disciplines; • Mentor undergraduate and graduate students and provide research experiences that prepare students for careers in agriculture, environmental science, natural-resource management, and related fields; • Teach undergraduate and graduate courses consistent with departmental and programmatic needs. Potential courses may include hydrology, water chemistry, biogeochemistry, watershed science, agricultural water management, or specialized courses in the candidate’s area of expertise; and contribute to the academic, research, Extension, and service missions of the Department of Natural Sciences, the Department of Agriculture, Food, and Resource Sciences, the Simon A. Haley School of Agriculture and Natural Sciences, and UMES. Required Qualifications: • Ph.D. from an accredited institution in Hydrology, Ecohydrology, Agricultural or Biological Engineering, Soil Science, Environmental Science, Geology, Natural Resources, Watershed Science, or a closely related discipline by the time of appointment; • Demonstrated expertise or strong potential for developing expertise in hydrologic, ecological, soil, or biogeochemical processes relevant to agricultural, coastal, estuarine, or watershed systems; • Evidence of research productivity and strong potential for scholarly achievement and development of an externally funded research program; • Evidence of potential for effective undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; • Effective communication and collaboration skills and an interest in interdisciplinary research. Physical Demands: • May require extended periods of standing, bending, stooping, sitting at desk. • May require lifting. • Requires communication with a variety of constituents externally and internally. • Requires operation of a variety of office equipment. Preferences: Preference will be given to candidates with one or more of the following: • Research experience addressing water, soil, nutrient, or biogeochemical processes in agricultural or working landscapes; • Experience with agricultural water-quality issues, nutrient management, drainage, irrigation, soil-water relations, salinity, or watershed processes; • Experience working in coastal plain, estuarine, or other hydrologically sensitive agricultural environments; • Experience using field-based, laboratory, sensor-based, geospatial, remote- sensing, modeling, or contemporary data-driven approaches relevant to ecohydrology; • Experience working with farmers, Extension personnel, conservation professionals, natural- resource managers, agencies, or other stakeholders; • Experience teaching at the college or university level; • Demonstrated success in, or strong potential for, obtaining competitive external funding; • Interest in collaborative and multidisciplinary approaches connecting agriculture, water resources, environmental quality, and climate resilience. Additional Job Details The successful candidate must be able to accept employment in the United States without employer immigration sponsorship Required Application Materials: All materials must be uploaded in order to receive full consideration at https://umd.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UMES A cover letter summarizing qualifications and interest in the position Curriculum vitae, including publication list Statement describing prior and current research and outlining future research plans (maximum 2 pages) Statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy (1 page) Statement of Extension philosophy describing the applicant’s approach to Extension, stakeholder engagement, or translation of research into practical applications (1 page) Unofficial graduate transcripts Names and contact information for 3 current professional references, including email addresses and telephone numbers Questions regarding the position should be directed to Dr. Jonathan Cumming, Professor and Chair, Department of Natural Sciences, Email: jrcumming@umes.edu Telephone: 410-651-6014. Best Consideration Date: Review of applications will begin on October 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled.   Posting Close Date: N/A   Open Until Filled: Yes   Department UMES-AGNS-Agriculture/Operations Worker Sub-Type Faculty Regular Salary Range $80,000 - $95,000 (Commensurate with qualifications and experience) EEO Statement UMES  is an EEO/AA employer and will not discriminate against any employee or applicant because of race, age, sex, color, sexual orientation, religion, national origin, marital status, genetic information, or political affiliation. Minorities, women, veterans, and persons with disabilities are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate must be able to show acceptable documentation establishing the right to accept employment in the United States of America without employer sponsorship. Diversity Statement The University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) is an equal access, equal opportunity institution fully committed to diversity in education and employment. All students, employees, and the campus community at UMES are valued, respected, and have the opportunity to receive an equitable experience in an inclusive, welcoming environment of openness and appreciation. Title IX Statement It is the policy of the University of Maryland Eastern Shore (UMES) to comply with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, which prohibits discrimination (including sexual harassment and sexual violence) based on sex in the University's educational programs and activities. Title IX also prohibits retaliation for asserting or otherwise participating in claims of sex discriminations or harassment."
    },
